  "notes": "Brass-and-funk-inflected band pop, windowed to the post-reunion era the entry names. The drums are a hybrid by the research's own account: \"played kit with a dry, forward backbeat and 16th hats; handclaps and tambourine doubling; the Bellion-produced records add programmed layers under the live kit\", so the humanize block is a played one but not a loose one, the snare is hard and dry with a short tail, `hihat.base` is 16th and the perc lanes are `tambourine` and `clave` at `downbeat`. No `bass808` block: \"no 808. Funk electric bass on syncopated 16ths as a co-hook\", which is `independent_riff` at 0.3 with slap-shaped cells and a high `anticipationProb`. Harmony is \"4-bar diatonic vamps with 7ths, muted guitar chanks and horn stabs\" over \"majors with dominant-7th funk colour\", so `seventh` carries close to half the weight and the mixolydian scale is weighted. The melody block encodes \"short-celled and call-and-response between brothers\" as `call_response` with a small `motifPitchCount`; the documented Bellion device, \"stacked choir-like vocal beds behind the lead\", is a high-density `sustain_pad` counter, and \"three-part sibling harmony written in close thirds with a tenor lead and a falsetto top\" is why the third share is raised. ⚠ `confidence` is medium and the research says the parameters are \"lane-derived\": every drum probability, the humanize numbers, the harmonic weights, the melody register and `bpm.mode` 115 (the midpoint of the stated 100—130 band) are INFERRED. ⚠ The entry's [LOW-CONF] mark on the sibling-harmony voicing claim is repeated here.",
